! sedov3d_amr.nml — Mera test fixture: 3-D Sedov–Taylor blast wave on an AMR grid
!
! Derived from RAMSES 2026.05 `tests/hydro/sedov3d/sedov3d.nml` — the configuration RAMSES's own
! test suite validates against. EXACTLY ONE thing is changed: &OUTPUT_PARAMS. Upstream writes a
! single output at its reference time; a Mera fixture wants several snapshots.
!
! Do NOT "simplify" this namelist. Two details are load-bearing and cost a day if rediscovered:
!
!   1. THE EXPLOSION IS AT THE ORIGIN, not the box centre. Region 2 has x_center=y_center=
!      z_center=0.0 while region 1 (the ambient medium) is centred at 0.5. With periodic
!      boundaries this evolves one octant of a sphere. Any analysis must measure radius from
!      [0,0,0] — measuring from the box centre yields a radius that DECREASES with time.
!      AND it must use MINIMUM-IMAGE distances: under periodicity the far corner (0.5,0.5,0.5)
!      IS an image of the origin, i.e. physically at the blast centre, while plain Euclidean
!      distance calls it 0.866. getvar(:r_sphere) is not periodic-aware, so compute
!          r = sqrt( sum_axes min(|d|, boxlen-|d|)^2 )
!      from :x/:y/:z. With Euclidean distance the fitted Sedov exponent comes out at 1.41
!      instead of 0.40; with minimum-image it is 0.375.
!
!   2. THE REFINEMENT MUST BE GEOMETRIC (r_refine/x_refine/exp_refine), not gradient-based.
!      condinit.f90 deposits a point region as  q = q + p_region*r/dx**ndim  — divided by the
!      CELL volume, so the blast strength depends on how refined the origin is AT INITIALISATION.
!      Geometric refinement guarantees fine cells there from step zero. Substituting a density-
!      gradient criterion (err_grad_d) refines nothing at t=0, because the initial density is
!      uniform: the energy lands on coarse cells, and instead of a sharp blast you get a weak
!      box-filling pressure bump that never exceeds rho ~ 2.4 (measured), versus the strong-shock
!      limit (gamma+1)/(gamma-1) = 6 for gamma = 1.4.
!
! WHY THESE SNAPSHOT TIMES. Sedov–Taylor gives a self-similar blast radius
!     R(t) = xi (E t^2 / rho)^(1/5),     so   d(log R)/d(log t) = 2/5
! The six times span a decade in t, over which R grows by 10^(2/5) ~ 2.5 — enough lever arm to
! FIT the exponent rather than check a single radius, turning this fixture into an analytic
! oracle. The last equals upstream's reference time 9.91e-3, so the final snapshot is directly
! comparable to the RAMSES test suite's own answer. output_00001 is written at t = 0, giving the
! untouched initial conditions too.
!
! MEASURED (RAMSES 2026.05, 8 MPI ranks, this laptop): 7 outputs, 11 MB total, < 1 s wall.
! Fitted d(logR)/d(logt) = 0.3749 vs theory 0.4 (-6.3 %), max residual 0.0057 dex, mass conserved
! to 12 digits. The -6 % and the peak density of 2.66 (vs the strong-shock limit 6) are both
! resolution effects: once the shock leaves the geometrically refined zone (r_refine=0.125) it is
! smeared over coarse cells. Widen r_refine to sharpen both, at the cost of fixture size.
!
! Run (3-D only — Mera raises "Program only works with 3D data!" on 1-D/2-D output):
!     mpirun -np 8 /tmp/rbuild/bin/ramses3d sedov3d_amr.nml > run.log 2>&1

&RUN_PARAMS
hydro=.true.
ncontrol=1
nrestart=0
nremap=0
nsubcycle=10*2
verbose=.false.
/

&AMR_PARAMS
levelmin=4
levelmax=7
ngridtot=40000
nexpand=0
boxlen=0.5
/

&INIT_PARAMS
nregion=2
region_type(1)='square'
region_type(2)='point'
x_center=0.5,0.0
y_center=0.5,0.0
z_center=0.5,0.0
length_x=10.0,1.0
length_y=10.0,1.0
length_z=10.0,1.0
exp_region=10.0,10.0
d_region=1.0,0.0
u_region=0.0,0.0
v_region=0.0,0.0
w_region=0.0,0.0
p_region=1e-5,0.4
/

&OUTPUT_PARAMS
noutput=6
tout=1.0d-3,2.0d-3,3.5d-3,5.5d-3,7.5d-3,9.91d-3
/

&HYDRO_PARAMS
gamma=1.4
courant_factor=0.8
scheme='muscl'
slope_type=8
riemann='hll'
/

&REFINE_PARAMS
interpol_var=2
interpol_type=1
err_grad_p=0.1
r_refine=0.125,0.0625,0.03125
x_refine=0.0625,0.0625
y_refine=0.0625,0.0625
z_refine=0.0625,0.0625
exp_refine=10,10.,10.
/
